    <!-- The three localpostgres* definitions below are the managed-RDBMS datasources. The Entity Engine
         configuration parser reads these attributes LITERALLY - there is no property-placeholder or
         system-property expansion anywhere in this file - so a deployment externalises them through
         docker/templates/postgres-entityengine.xml, which the container entry point renders into
         /ofbiz/config/entityengine.xml (first on the runtime classpath) when OFBIZ_POSTGRES_HOST is set. That
         template documents each placeholder at the attribute it occupies, and its datasource definitions are
         derived from these three, so the two stay in step.
         MAINTENANCE RULE: never write an at-sign delimited placeholder name anywhere in THIS file, not even in
         a comment, and describe placeholders by the attribute they occupy as the comments here do. The template
         is a sed source and a name written in prose would be substituted there too.
         LOCAL-DEVELOPMENT PLACEHOLDERS: the host, database, username and password below are the long standing
         defaults for a PostgreSQL installed on the developer's own machine, exactly as the mysql, oracle and
         other dialect definitions beside them keep theirs. They are placeholders, not deployment credentials:
         a deployed container never uses them as written, because the entry point renders the template with the
         host, database, username and password taken from the environment and puts the result first on the
         classpath. A developer pointing a delegator at these three edits them locally, or supplies a
         jdbc-password-lookup naming a property in a passwords.properties that is not committed.
         The secrets that ARE externalised by this refactor - the admin key, the forgot-password key and the
         JWT signing key - live in framework/security/config/security.properties and
         framework/start/src/main/resources/org/apache/ofbiz/base/start/start.properties, which ship without a
         value and are injected from the environment at deploy time; none of them belongs here.
         TLS: the URIs below leave sslmode at the pgJDBC default, because they address 127.0.0.1 - a loopback
         connection that never crosses a network - and a checkout provisions no certificate authority for
         anything stronger. Every connection that DOES cross a network is a rendered one, and the entry point
         puts sslmode and sslrootcert into each rendered URI from OFBIZ_POSTGRES_SSLMODE and
         OFBIZ_POSTGRES_SSLROOTCERT, defaulting the prod profile to verify-full and refusing to start without a
         readable CA file - pgJDBC does not consult the JVM trust store, it reads sslrootcert. A local install
         that does reach PostgreSQL over a network should add sslmode=verify-full and sslrootcert to the URI
         here. Enforce it from the server as well, with a hostssl rule in pg_hba.conf or rds.force_ssl on a
         managed instance: a server-side rule cannot be forgotten the way a per-client setting can.
         EVERY NETWORK WAIT IS BOUNDED: each URI below pins the pgJDBC deadlines and each pool below pins its
         borrow wait, because the value that applies when either is absent is not a deadline at all. pgJDBC
         leaves socketTimeout and loginTimeout at 0, which means NO LIMIT, so a thread that reaches a database
         which has stopped answering blocks on the socket read for as long as the kernel keeps the connection
         open - and that strands the startup entity check and the readiness probe's query as much as a request
         thread. Independently, InlineJdbc reads an absent pool-sleeptime as 300000 ms, five minutes, which is
         longer than any load-balancer health-check timeout, so an exhausted pool would present as a hung
         instance. socketTimeout bounds a SINGLE socket read rather than a statement, so the value is generous
         enough that no normal statement, data load or schema check is affected while still being finite. There
         is deliberately no queryTimeout: it would apply to EVERY statement and abort legitimately long work
         such as a data load or a large report. pool-jdbc-test-stmt with test-while-idle stops the pool handing
         out a connection that died while idle, the ordinary outcome of a managed-database failover; without a
         validation query DBCP performs no validation at all, whichever test-* flag is set. Validating during
         the eviction runs that already happen costs a request nothing, so test-on-borrow - one extra round trip
         on every borrow - stays false. The template carries the same deadlines and the same pool settings, so a
         rendered deployment and a non-container install that points a delegator at these definitions are
         bounded identically. Only these three managed definitions carry them: the MySQL, Oracle and other
         dialect definitions are preserved exactly as they are for portability. -->
    <!-- GATED SCHEMA INITIALIZATION: this managed-RDBMS run mode issues NO startup DDL, so a serving instance
         needs no DDL privileges and a whole fleet can scale out without racing on schema changes. The schema is
         applied exclusively by a separate one-shot init execution driven by OFBIZ_SCHEMA_INIT=true, which the
         container entry point runs by rendering the template with check-on-start and add-missing-on-start both
         true, starting OFBiz once so the Entity Engine creates the tables the entity model declares (the pass
         is additive: it creates missing tables and columns and never drops), re-rendering with both false, and
         exiting without ever reaching the serving command - so the DDL privilege that execution needs never
         belongs to a long-lived process. It invokes the loader with "readers=none", because creating the
         delegator is what applies the schema and an empty reader list then loads nothing.
         Note how Datasource.java parses the two flags: check-on-start is read as !"false".equals(attribute), so
         an absent attribute means TRUE and the literal false below is what actually disables the startup schema
         check, while add-missing-on-start is read as "true".equals(attribute), so its false is explicit rather
         than required.
         Schema truth remains the entity model - no Flyway, Liquibase, schema-version table or hand-written DDL.
         The embedded localh2* datasources deliberately keep both flags enabled for zero-configuration local
         development, where the file-based database belongs to the single JVM that opens it. -->
    <datasource name="localpostgres"
            helper-class="org.apache.ofbiz.entity.datasource.GenericHelperDAO"
            schema-name="public"
            field-type-name="postgres"
            check-on-start="false"
            add-missing-on-start="false"
            use-fk-initially-deferred="false"
            alias-view-columns="false"
            join-style="ansi"
            use-binary-type-for-blob="true"
            use-order-by-nulls="true"
            offset-style="limit"
            result-fetch-size="50">

        <read-data reader-name="tenant"/>
        <read-data reader-name="seed"/>
        <read-data reader-name="seed-initial"/>
        <read-data reader-name="demo"/>
        <read-data reader-name="ext"/>
        <read-data reader-name="ext-test"/>
        <read-data reader-name="ext-demo"/>
        <inline-jdbc
                jdbc-driver="org.postgresql.Driver"
                jdbc-uri="jdbc:postgresql://127.0.0.1:5432/ofbiz?connectTimeout=10&amp;socketTimeout=60&amp;loginTimeout=30&amp;cancelSignalTimeout=10&amp;tcpKeepAlive=true"
                jdbc-username="ofbiz"
                jdbc-password="ofbiz"
                isolation-level="ReadCommitted"
                pool-minsize="2"
                pool-maxsize="250"
                time-between-eviction-runs-millis="600000"
                pool-sleeptime="20000"
                pool-jdbc-test-stmt="SELECT 1"
                test-while-idle="true"
                test-on-borrow="false"/>

    </datasource>
    <datasource name="localpostgresolap"
            helper-class="org.apache.ofbiz.entity.datasource.GenericHelperDAO"
            schema-name="public"
            field-type-name="postgres"
            check-on-start="false"
